            {
                "entry_id": "d8d09979-c700-361d-a4d5-07ad3135103d",
                "word": "mahogany",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "d42aca73-2381-46ea-b311-0cdd3c360fe9",
                        "entry_id": "d8d09979-c700-361d-a4d5-07ad3135103d",
                        "tags": [],
                        "form": "mahogani"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "7d135d00-71c7-308e-a6d2-9e664b554e8b",
                        "entry_id": "d8d09979-c700-361d-a4d5-07ad3135103d",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Bois d’acajou."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "81708a45-5a99-47e6-ae35-b4b837849494",
                                "sense_id": "7d135d00-71c7-308e-a6d2-9e664b554e8b",
                                "text": "Elle ouvrit un délicieux petit secrétaire en pulpe de mahogany et en tira un épais dossier sous une chemise verte."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "16ebe81c-13d3-4336-b454-4cfc27535be6",
                                "sense_id": "7d135d00-71c7-308e-a6d2-9e664b554e8b",
                                "text": "À ce qu’il m'a raconté […] elle avait un grand lit, recouvert d’un édredon rouge sous lequel un drap décoré avec des dessins d’oiseaux donnait l’illusion de rentrer dans un ciel. […] Un beau bouquet de fleurs du pays ajoutait ses couleurs sur une table de nuit en mahogany."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "21aa8de2-2930-4e69-8d64-54a564021e4f",
                        "source_entry_id": "d8d09979-c700-361d-a4d5-07ad3135103d",
                        "source_sense_id": "7d135d00-71c7-308e-a6d2-9e664b554e8b",
                        "source_sense_description": "Bois d’acajou",
                        "target_lang_word": "mahogany",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "mahogany",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "b77f3ade-6391-3faa-a0db-c83e43734226",
                                        "entry_id": "bf0e71c3-8746-36c3-85ff-dbd8abf72c67",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The valuable wood of any of various tropical American evergreen trees, of the genus Swietenia, mostly used to make furniture."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3efa9bd1-a5bf-486c-be57-a5ba360547fa",
                                                "sense_id": "b77f3ade-6391-3faa-a0db-c83e43734226",
                                                "text": "A very neat old woman, still in her good outdoor coat and best beehive hat, was sitting at a polished mahogany table on whose surface there were several scored scratches so deep that a triangular piece of the veneer had come cleanly away[…]."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e863b508-2b7a-48cb-b701-5021caae2840",
                                                "sense_id": "b77f3ade-6391-3faa-a0db-c83e43734226",
                                                "text": "In 2003, at Neal Auction Company in New Orleans, an 1810s mahogany armoire inlaid with ribbons and vines brought $140,000 (the presale estimate was $30,000 to $50,000)."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "5c0d6661-34df-3b02-bbfb-db78cd37873b",
                                        "entry_id": "bf0e71c3-8746-36c3-85ff-dbd8abf72c67",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Any of the trees from which such wood comes."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "e2064908-268a-32eb-88ef-1f082d6489bb",
                                        "entry_id": "bf0e71c3-8746-36c3-85ff-dbd8abf72c67",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "(by extension) Any of various trees which resemble those of the genus Swietenia."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "af595180-40b3-3c23-b765-b41792c3f757",
                                        "entry_id": "bf0e71c3-8746-36c3-85ff-dbd8abf72c67",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A Cornish drink made from gin and treacle."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "regional",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "88f31db2-167b-420d-8517-b213267da2b6",
                                                "sense_id": "af595180-40b3-3c23-b765-b41792c3f757",
                                                "text": "William Murdoch […] produced a bottle of port; but I chose mahogany (two parts gin and one part treacle, which Lord Eliot made us at Sir Joshua Reynolds's as a Cornish liquor, but it seems they make it also with brandy, and often add porter to it)."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "82ec8664-5063-38a5-bf69-72fe7e42f919",
                                        "entry_id": "bf0e71c3-8746-36c3-85ff-dbd8abf72c67",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A reddish-brown color, like that of mahogany wood."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "310b8885-2f06-4cc4-ac5b-4f0e64adb3a5",
                                                "sense_id": "82ec8664-5063-38a5-bf69-72fe7e42f919",
                                                "text": "Better she, my dear, than a black Mrs. Sedley, and a dozen of mahogany grandchildren."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "f80a7f07-d5f1-33fc-8548-77791f6c4866",
                                        "entry_id": "bf0e71c3-8746-36c3-85ff-dbd8abf72c67",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A table made from mahogany wood; a dining table."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "colloquial",
                                            "countable",
                                            "obsolete",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a5dc5c9d-dd35-43b9-966e-8724d314652a",
                                                "sense_id": "f80a7f07-d5f1-33fc-8548-77791f6c4866",
                                                "text": "Poets eat and drink without stint — and seldom at their own cost — for what man of mark or likelihood in the moneyed world is there, who is not eager to get their legs under his mahogany?"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "bd76ed02-ec39-493f-b531-333bbdacfec1",
                                                "sense_id": "f80a7f07-d5f1-33fc-8548-77791f6c4866",
                                                "text": "Yet habit—strange thing! what cannot habit accomplish?—Gayer sallies, more merry mirth, better jokes, and brighter repartees, you never heard over your mahogany […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
